ChatGPT just learned how to turn your photos and prompts into real animated gifts you can tweak frame by frame. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, Episode 218. After we unpack that, we'll walk through how a face stays itself from frame to frame. A University of Toronto survey you can take and quick bits. OpenAI, the decoder reports, built this on a new picture model called Images 2.6. A model is the train brain behind the tool. The thing that learned patterns from huge piles of pictures.

00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:34.860
You type a prompt, which is just a sentence that describes what you want, or you upload a photo.

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:41.880
The system then strings several still pictures into a short clip that loops, playing over and over like a sticker in a group chat.

00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:49.000
Each still picture is called a frame, and a row of frames becomes motion the way a paper flipbook does when you thumb the pages.

00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:55.680
Picture handing your phone a tiny animation studio and asking it to draw every page of a flipbook from one sentence.

00:00:55.680 --> 00:01:00.820
That used to mean expensive software and hours of work for even a few seconds of movement.

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:06.940
Now it is a few taps on a free or paid chat GPT account, on the web, on a desktop, or

00:01:06.940 --> 00:01:07.660
on your phone.

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:12.980
Codex users get it as well while it rolls out, and Codex is OpenAI's helper that writes

00:01:12.980 --> 00:01:14.160
computer code with you.

00:01:14.620 --> 00:01:18.720
Students can turn a history report into a moving scene instead of a frozen poster.

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:22.640
Artists can test a quick idea before committing to a long drawing.

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:26.400
Friends can send each other custom reaction clips they made themselves.

00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:29.960
The way people already pass around looping jokes and group chats.

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:32.740
Still pictures are growing into moving ones,

00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:36.060
and that shift can touch school projects and social posts alike.

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:39.660
OpenAI also released two new models for the API.

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:43.780
The toolkit developers use to plug these picture tools into other apps.

00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:47.060
API stands for Application Programming Interface,

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:51.320
which is a fancy way of saying a plug that lets one program talk to another.

00:01:51.320 --> 00:01:57.320
One model is called GPT Image 2.6 Flair, and it is built for faster generation.

00:01:57.880 --> 00:02:00.880
The other is called Sunburst, and it is built for higher quality.

00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:05.760
Both run about 50% quicker than before, so waiting around shrinks a lot.

00:02:06.340 --> 00:02:11.360
People now create well over 4 billion images every week through chat GPT and that developer

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:11.880
toolkit.

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:17.040
Four billion pictures is more than anyone could scroll in a lifetime, like every phone

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:20.160
in a huge stadium taking photos at once, over and over.

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:25.640
You can save a reference picture of yourself, a pet or a character, then reuse it in later

00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:27.540
creations without uploading it again.

00:02:28.120 --> 00:02:33.720
Open chat GPT and type, make a gif of a cat dancing on a skateboard, or upload a photo

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:36.160
of your pet and ask it to animate the pet waving.

00:02:36.760 --> 00:02:41.060
Then drag the sliders to change the speed and watch whether the motion feels smooth or

00:02:41.060 --> 00:02:41.540
jumpy.

00:02:42.448 --> 00:02:46.508
Keeping a cartoon face identical from one drawing to the next is the oldest headache

00:02:46.508 --> 00:02:47.608
in homemade animation.

00:02:47.608 --> 00:02:53.268
paper, you sketch the same character on several pages and the hair or the shirt quietly

00:02:53.268 --> 00:02:54.648
changes if your hand slips.

00:02:55.168 --> 00:02:59.868
An AI has a tougher job because it paints every new frame from scratch instead of tracing

00:02:59.868 --> 00:03:00.748
the last page.

00:03:01.188 --> 00:03:06.488
It has to remember the first look in detail, hair color, shirt pattern, even the way the

00:03:06.488 --> 00:03:07.108
shadows fall.

00:03:07.568 --> 00:03:12.188
Then it has to copy that look into each new frame without drifting, like holding a tune

00:03:12.188 --> 00:03:13.708
in your head while you keep singing.

00:03:13.708 --> 00:03:20.988
It treats the first picture as a strong reference, almost like a teacher glancing back at the original sketch while a student keeps drawing.

00:03:21.648 --> 00:03:25.528
When that memory holds, your eye accepts the motion as the same person moving.

00:03:26.068 --> 00:03:30.668
When it slips, the character changes height or grows an extra finger, and the spell breaks.

00:03:31.188 --> 00:03:33.228
Like a video game glitch, you cannot unsee.

00:03:33.828 --> 00:03:41.428
The new model is better at this memory trick than older ones, so the loops feel like real short animations instead of random pictures taped together.

00:03:41.428 --> 00:03:45.808
That skill comes from training that treats the starting image as a fixed anchor.

00:03:46.288 --> 00:03:50.528
The way a boat stays put when you tie it to a dock, instead of starting fresh each

00:03:50.528 --> 00:03:50.888
time.

00:03:51.448 --> 00:03:55.828
Small details survive the trip, a jacket zipper, the shape of a pet's ear.

00:03:56.328 --> 00:04:00.528
The same dock and rope trick is why a saved reference photo can keep showing up later

00:04:00.528 --> 00:04:02.588
without the character wandering off model.

00:04:03.188 --> 00:04:07.868
Once you see it as a memory problem, those little looping clips stop feeling like magic

00:04:07.868 --> 00:04:11.408
and start feeling like a homework check the model finally learned to pass.

00:04:12.349 --> 00:04:15.949
Researchers at the University of Toronto described in a Reddit post

00:04:15.949 --> 00:04:21.189
want everyday people to describe how they talk to chat bots for writing, advice, or searching.

00:04:21.909 --> 00:04:24.909
Chat bots are those chat apps that answer you in plain language.

00:04:25.469 --> 00:04:28.749
The same kind of tool many of you already use for homework or fun.

00:04:29.309 --> 00:04:34.269
The survey is anonymous, takes up to 30 minutes, and you do not need any technical background.

00:04:34.269 --> 00:04:39.709
The questions focus on daily experiences with your favorite chatbot, and on what you think

00:04:39.709 --> 00:04:41.109
it can and cannot do.

00:04:41.669 --> 00:04:47.149
Your answers stay private because no names or IP addresses get collected, and an IP address

00:04:47.149 --> 00:04:50.009
is just the number that identifies a device on the internet.

00:04:50.609 --> 00:04:54.689
Researchers only see patterns in how people use these tools, not who you are.

00:04:55.269 --> 00:04:59.849
Think of it as dropping a note in a giant suggestion box, so the next version of these

00:04:59.849 --> 00:05:02.609
apps matches how real people actually talk to them.

00:05:02.609 --> 00:05:08.109
Open Reddit on your phone or laptop, search for the University of Toronto Chatbot Survey,

00:05:08.509 --> 00:05:09.749
and answer the questions.

00:05:10.169 --> 00:05:14.009
If you are under 18, you may need a parent's help, and then you are done.

